# Meta Software

This project is a Node.js + Express API for user authentication and post management. It uses MongoDB as the database and stores users and posts in collections managed by Mongoose models.

## 1. Project Overview

The application exposes a simple backend for:

- creating accounts and logging in users
- generating JWT-based authentication tokens
- creating, reading, updating, and deleting posts
- validating incoming request data

## 2. Tech Stack

- Node.js
- Express.js
- MongoDB
- Mongoose
- JWT
- bcrypt
- Joi

## 3. Local Setup

### Prerequisites

- Node.js installed
- MongoDB running locally or a MongoDB Atlas connection string

### Install dependencies

```bash
npm install
```

### Configure environment variables

Create a `.env` file in the project root with the following values:

```env
PORT=5000
MONGODB_URI=mongodb://localhost:27017/meta-software
JWT_SECRET=your_super_secret_key
```

### Run the project

Start the server in development mode:

```bash
npm run dev
```

Or start normally:

```bash
npm start
```

The server will run on the port defined in `PORT`.

## 4. Implemented Endpoints

### Authentication

| Method | Route            | Description             |
| ------ | ---------------- | ----------------------- |
| POST   | `/auth/register` | Register a new user     |
| POST   | `/auth/login`    | Log in an existing user |

### Posts

| Method | Route        | Description                                           |
| ------ | ------------ | ----------------------------------------------------- |
| GET    | `/posts`     | Get all posts                                         |
| POST   | `/posts`     | Create a new post (requires authentication)           |
| PUT    | `/posts/:id` | Update a post (requires authentication and ownership) |
| DELETE | `/posts/:id` | Delete a post (requires authentication and ownership) |

### Auth Header

Protected routes expect a Bearer token in the Authorization header:

```http
authorization: Bearer <token>
```

## 5. Database Choice

This project uses MongoDB because it is a good fit for a lightweight backend API with flexible document-based data.

Why MongoDB:

- easy to store user and post records as documents
- works well with JavaScript/Node.js applications
- fast to set up for small to medium-sized APIs
- integrates smoothly with Mongoose for schema validation and query handling

MongoDB is especially suitable here because the data is not highly relational, and the application mainly deals with simple user and post records.

## 6. Project Structure

```text
.
├── Controllers/
│   ├── posts.js
│   └── users.js
├── Middlewares/
│   ├── auth.js
│   └── Validations/
